      "side": 1,
      "message": "Thanks for the comment! I don\u0027t think `wb_ctl-\u003edone_reqs` can contain elements when `release_wb_ctl(wb_ctl)` is called, so there is no memory leak or warning trigger. Here is why:\n\n1. `release_wb_ctl` is only called after `zram_writeback_slots` exits.\n2. `zram_writeback_slots` always blockingly waits for `wb_ctl-\u003enum_inflight` to become `0` before exiting (both in normal and error paths):\n   ```c\n   while (atomic_read(\u0026wb_ctl-\u003enum_inflight) \u003e 0) {\n       wait_event(wb_ctl-\u003edone_wait, !list_empty(\u0026wb_ctl-\u003edone_reqs));\n       err \u003d zram_complete_done_reqs(zram, wb_ctl);\n       ...\n   }\n   ```\n3. `wb_ctl-\u003enum_inflight` is only decremented in `zram_complete_done_reqs` *after* the request is successfully popped from `wb_ctl-\u003edone_reqs`.\n4. Therefore, `num_inflight` represents `in_flight` + `in_done_reqs` + `popped_but_not_yet_decremented`. When `num_inflight` reaches `0`, it is mathematically guaranteed that all completed requests have been popped from `done_reqs` and processed (moved back to `idle_reqs` and freed).\n5. If `zram_writeback_endio` runs concurrently with `release_wb_ctl` (which can only happen between `spin_unlock_irqrestore(\u0026wb_ctl-\u003edone_lock)` and `rcu_read_unlock()` in `zram_writeback_endio`), the request has already been popped and processed, so `done_reqs` is empty and `num_inflight` is `0`.\n6. Since `rcu_read_lock()` is held during this window, `kfree_rcu(wb_ctl, rcu)` ensures that `wb_ctl` is not freed until `rcu_read_unlock()` is called, preventing the UAF on `wake_up`.\n\nThus, `done_reqs` is guaranteed to be empty, and no requests will be leaked.",
      "revId": "d2b2a06b31dd585472c91e1d4496f0a4fb2adbc3",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
